<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter11_why-programs-need-error-handling">
    <title>Why Programs Need Error Handling</title>

    <p>
        A program can be syntactically correct and still fail when it runs.
        Users may provide unexpected input, a function may receive an invalid
        argument, or a needed resource may not be available. Reliable programs
        anticipate these situations and respond deliberately.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_three-kinds-of-errors">
        <title>Three Kinds of Programming Errors</title>

        <p>
            It is useful to distinguish errors by when they are discovered and
            by whether the programmer or the running program can respond to
            them.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter11_error-kinds">
            <title>Common kinds of programming errors</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="20%"/>
                <col width="20%"/>
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="30%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Kind of error</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>When it appears</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Example</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Typical response</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Compile-time error</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Before the program runs</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A missing semicolon or incompatible type</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Correct the source code.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Runtime error</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>While the program runs</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Opening a file that does not exist</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Detect the failure and respond safely.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Logic error</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>While the program runs</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Using the wrong formula for an average</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Test the result and correct the algorithm.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Error handling mainly addresses runtime situations in which the
            program cannot complete an operation as requested. It cannot repair
            a syntax error, and it cannot automatically recognize every logic
            error.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_invalid-conditions">
        <title>Invalid Conditions Are Part of Program Design</title>

        <p>
            A function should document and protect its assumptions. A required
            assumption is called a <term>precondition</term>. A result that the
            function promises after it succeeds is a <term>postcondition</term>.
            A rule that must remain true for an object is an
            <term>invariant</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, an average requires a nonzero number of values. The
            function below makes that requirement visible to its caller by
            returning a success value. It does not yet use exceptions; the next
            sections will introduce exceptions as another way to report failure.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_report-average-status">
            <title>Reporting whether an operation succeeded</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_report-average-status"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

bool try_calculate_average(int total, int count, double& average) {
    if (count == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    average = static_cast<double>(total) / count;
    return true;
}

int main() {
    double average{};

    if (try_calculate_average(85, 0, average)) {
        std::cout << average << '\n';
    }
    else {
        std::cout << "No average is available" << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The caller must remember to check the Boolean result before using
            <c>average</c>. This approach can work well, but every caller must
            remember to check the status. Exception handling provides another
            design in which the failure can travel to code prepared to handle
            it.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_normal-versus-exceptional-flow">
        <title>Normal Decisions and Exceptional Conditions</title>

        <p>
            Not every unusual situation should become an exception. A program
            should use ordinary control flow for expected choices, such as
            asking whether a user wants to continue. An exception is more
            appropriate when an operation cannot fulfill its responsibility and
            the failure must be handled somewhere else.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter11_normal-exceptional-flow">
            <title>Choosing a response to a condition</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="32%"/>
                <col width="40%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Situation</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Usually appropriate</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Reason</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>A loop has reached its normal stopping condition.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Ordinary control flow</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>This is an expected part of the algorithm.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>A function receives a value that violates its contract.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>An error result or an exception</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The caller must be informed that the operation did not succeed.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>A file cannot be opened.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>An error result or an exception</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The requested operation cannot continue normally.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>A calculation produces the wrong formula.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Testing and debugging</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>This is a logic error, not automatically an exception.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The English word <q>exception</q> means something that falls outside
            the normal pattern. In C++, an exception is a mechanism for
            transferring control from the code that detects an exceptional
            condition to code that is prepared to respond. The next section
            introduces the syntax for that transfer.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_error-handling-trace">
        <title>Tracing a Successful and Unsuccessful Call</title>

        <p>
            The visualization shows that the caller examines the status before
            deciding whether to use the output value.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter11_error-status"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Abool%20try_calculate_average%28int%20total%2C%20int%20count%2C%20double%26%20average%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20if%20%28count%20%3D%3D%200%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20return%20false%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20average%20%3D%20static_cast%3Cdouble%3E%28total%29%20%2F%20count%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%20true%3B%0A%7D%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20double%20average%7B%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20bool%20succeeded%20%3D%20try_calculate_average%2885%2C%200%2C%20average%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20succeeded%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=560&amp;codeDivWidth=450&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;mode=display&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="4:3">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter11_error_exercise-kinds">
        <statement>
            <p>Which situation is a runtime error?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>A function returns the wrong answer because its formula is incorrect.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>That is a logic error, not a runtime failure such as a missing file.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A closing brace is missing.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The compiler can detect this before the program runs.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>An average uses the wrong formula but still produces a number.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>That is a logic error: the program runs but computes the wrong result.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>A program cannot open a requested input file.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The program has compiled, but an operation fails while it runs.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A variable has a misleading name.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A misleading name is a design or readability problem, not necessarily a runtime failure.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_error_exercise-precondition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In <c>try_calculate_average</c>, what condition must be true
                before the function can calculate an average?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The caller must create a file.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This function performs a calculation and does not use a file.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>total</c> must be positive.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>An average can be calculated from a negative total; the divisor is the relevant condition.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p><c>count</c> must be negative.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>The function rejects zero, so it requires a nonzero count; this example uses zero as the invalid case.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>average</c> must already contain a value.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The function writes the calculated result into <c>average</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>count</c> must not be zero.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Division by zero must be prevented before the calculation.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_error_parsons-status-check">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the lines so the caller checks the status before using the average.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>double average{};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>if (try_calculate_average(85, 5, average)) {</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; average &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_error_exercise-response">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which response is generally most appropriate when a loop
                reaches its expected stopping condition?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>Throw an exception whenever normal termination occurs.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>An expected stopping condition belongs in ordinary control flow.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Use ordinary control flow.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>An expected stopping condition is part of normal algorithm behavior.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Ignore the condition and continue forever.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The loop should respond to its normal stopping condition.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Always throw an exception.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Exceptions are not normally needed for expected control flow.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Terminate the operating system.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A normal loop ending is not a system failure.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_error_exercise-runtime-examples">
        <statement>
            <p>Select all situations that are discovered while a program is running.</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A closing brace is missing from the source code.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This is a compile-time problem found before normal execution.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>A requested input file is missing when the program tries to open it.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This is a runtime condition discovered during execution.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A variable has a misleading name but the program still compiles and runs.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A misleading name is a design or readability problem, not necessarily a runtime condition.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>A function receives an invalid value from a caller while the program runs.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This condition is discovered during execution and can be handled deliberately.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A calculation uses the wrong formula but still produces a number.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This is a logic error, not necessarily a runtime failure.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>
